WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.460 --> 00:00:03.500
Phillip: So how do you go about
managing innovation and change?

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:08.220
It’s all about planning, monitoring,
and using review techniques.

00:00:08.929 --> 00:00:13.030
Maddy: There are lots of different models you
can use to help you manage innovation and change.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:18.219
In this video we’re going to take a look
at three common models: Gantt Charts,

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:21.360
the PDSA model, and the SMART model.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:22.919
Phillip: Gantt Charts

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:28.660
A Gantt chart is a form of bar chart that
represents each activity in a project.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:34.780
This could be installing a new IT system or
training teams in new work methods, and the

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:39.589
chart represents the amount of time each
activity will take to complete, and the tasks

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.950
that need to be done for that completion.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.239
Maddy: By creating a Gantt Chart, you are
mapping out everything that needs to be done

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:53.580
to achieve the change or innovation identifying
all the tasks and activities, what they

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.129
rely on, and when they need to be completed.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:02.190
For example, a major activity in an
innovation project might be training everyone

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.519
in a team to use a new computer system.

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:08.630
However, this can’t happen until
that system has been installed.

00:01:09.509 --> 00:01:12.480
But is installing the system the
first thing that needs to be done?

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:19.020
Phillip: It might not be, does the organisation’s
IT system need to give their approval first?

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:23.020
Are there going to have to be other changes
applied to the whole computer system

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.489
before the new system can be installed?

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:31.060
A Gantt Chart can help identify the critical
tasks that need to be completed to keep the

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.780
innovation project on track and on schedule.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:35.870
Maddy: PDSA

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:44.840
The next model we are going to look at is PDSA
and this stands for: Plan, Do, Study, Act.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.790
It’s a model for testing the changes you
want to implement before fully committing,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.240
and can help you spot potential problem
areas before you start the roll-out.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.270
Let’s have a look at the four stages in order:

00:01:58.219 --> 00:02:02.959
• Phillip: Plan – before the change takes
place, you need to spend time planning it,

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:05.229
so you know what you’re doing, and when

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.000
• Maddy: Do – make the change

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:14.919
• Phillip: Study – compare data from before or after
the change; for example, if you’re trying a new

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.519
sales strategy, are you attracting new customers?

00:02:18.009 --> 00:02:22.920
• Maddy: Act – proceed with fully implementing
the change, or go back to the planning stage

00:02:23.619 --> 00:02:28.730
The PDSA model also provides you with three
questions that can help to identify what the

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:30.530
change you’re actually making is going to be:

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.780
• What are we trying to accomplish?

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:34.850
- This is called the aims statement.

00:02:35.429 --> 00:02:38.149
• Phillip: How will we know if
the change is an improvement?

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.600
- This helps you identify what
data you need to collect.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:45.020
• Maddy: What change can we make
that will result in improvement?

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:47.790
-This is where you identify the change itself.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:48.890
Phillip: SMART

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.360
Our final model is SMART, and this
stands for: Specific, Measurable,

00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:56.870
Attainable, Realistic, Time bound.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:03.130
Maddy: SMART is a model you might have heard of
before as it is used in many different fields.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:08.709
It helps to specify the targets and objectives
you’re trying to achieve through the innovation.

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:12.410
So, let’s look at each part
of SMART in more detail.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:17.599
• Phillip: Specific –This means you
clearly define the goal you’re pursuing.

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:21.530
You should avoid making the goal
too general, as this makes it harder

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:22.820
to know if it’s been achieved.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.799
• Maddy: Measurable – You should define how
you’ll know that the goal has been achieved.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:32.689
You can see that it’s easier to do that with
a specific goal, rather than a general goal.

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:37.150
• Phillip: Attainable – The goal
you’ve defined has to be possible!

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.970
There isn’t much point in putting
time and energy behind a goal that

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:42.540
has little chance of being achieved.

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:48.870
• Maddy: Realistic – A goal may be possible
but still not actually likely to be achieved.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:52.570
Make sure it’s realistic that the
goal can actually be attained.

00:03:53.129 --> 00:03:56.910
• Phillip: Time-bound – there should be a
clear timeline for achieving the goal.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:01.180
This helps focus activity and makes
the plan more likely to succeed.

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:05.799
Maddy: SMART allows you to really think
about the kinds of goals that you want to

00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:08.610
achieve in order to successfully innovate.

00:04:09.339 --> 00:04:13.239
Phillip: There’s a wealth of information
online about these methods, along with

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:17.580
many others and some may feel more
useful and relevant directly to you.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:21.850
So, take the time to do some additional
research and to deepen your knowledge

00:04:21.889 --> 00:04:23.689
on managing innovation and change.

